{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790342206,"nonce":"5V1OG4IgCH+KcBv3PQPwyN62Zjs=","title":"Lua: every array element and Any slot is a separate heap box","message":"An integer or float in an array part is a pointer to a 48 B DynamicBox block plus the 8 B slot: 56 B per element against 16 in Lua 5.4 and 8 in LuaJIT. A table boxed into Any gets a separate 32 B zb_box_instance box each time; a 2-element positional table {a, b} costs about 270 B in 7 blocks (Lua about 112); strings are not interned.\n\nEvidence (Mac, pool slab-class census on 100k-element micro programs): int and float arrays one 48 B box per element; {x,y,z} rows 80 + 32 B; {i, i+1} about 270 B; string arrays about 157 B carved per element. pipeline zt at exit: 2050 slabs of 48 B (128 MB, 2.8M scalar boxes) + 24 MB of 32 B + 26 MB array buffers = 179 MB against lua5.4's 75.7 MB process. records: 294 slabs of 32 B (18.4 MB) are Any boxes of its rows. tokenize live 16.1 MB of strings. Shaped tables themselves are small (80 B for a 3-field node against about 160 in Lua and LuaJIT).\n\nSavings: pipeline about 130 MB of its 185 MB gap; records 20 to 25; tokenize 10 to 14.\n\nFix: typed array parts (an i64 or f64 list when ShapeInfo.element is Int or Float, migrating to Any on the first other store); a table carries its own box header in front of it, as BOXED_PREFIX strings do, so Table to Any is an address computation; exact-size positional constructors; short-string interning. The DynamicBox ABI (data at 8, dropper at 16) stays. Package MEM-lua-unboxed-arrays; interning coordinates with LO-string-identity, constructors with LO-huge-constructors.","files":null}]}